Synchronized output (DEC private mode 2026) in mosh-server.

An application brackets a redraw with "begin frame" (CSI ? 2026 h) and "end
frame" (CSI ? 2026 l) so that the terminal never shows it half drawn, after
asking whether the terminal supports it (DECRQM, CSI ? 2026 $ p). mosh answered
nothing, so applications bracketed nothing, and mosh-server's state syncs, which
run on their own clock, could land in the middle of a redraw.

Zellij shows why that matters. Every `zellij pipe` or `zellij action`, even a
read-only one, makes it redraw the whole screen: hide the cursor, rewrite every
cell in many 1 KB writes, show the cursor again. A sync from the middle carries
"cursor hidden", and the next one, a send interval later, "cursor shown". Over
mosh the cursor blinked off for about 100 ms several times a minute, whenever an
agent hook called zellij.

This answers DECRQM for mode 2026, tracks the mode in DrawState, and has
mosh-server hold the state it publishes while a frame is open. The state goes
out when the frame ends, or after 250 ms if the application never ends it; each
new "begin frame" gets its own 250 ms. Other modes stay unanswered, as before.
The mode is never sent to the client, so DrawState::operator== leaves it out.

Only mosh-server's behaviour changes. A patched server with a stock client is
fixed in full.

Upstream has no implementation; the feature request is open:
  https://github.com/mobile-shell/mosh/issues/1312

